 "meta": {"name": "AgfaPhoto Vista plus 200", "maker": "Agfa", "kind": "colour-negative", "cine": false, "iso": 200, "balance": "daylight", "log_h_ref": null, "densitometry": "Status M", "process": "CN-16", "grain": {"rms": null}, "print_on": null, "document": null, "date": null, "notes": ["AgfaPhoto 'Product Information Vista plus 200' (English), page 4 plots, laid out like Fujifilm's bulletins (the sheet calls the film 'Vista plus 200 (CN)', a daylight-type ISO 200 colour negative film with New Super Uniform Fine Grain technology). The sheet prints no publication number and no date and no RMS granularity value: document and date are null here (the PDF's embedded metadata, not printed text, gives a creation date of 2009-11-12), grain is null.", "Characteristic curves: daylight exposure 1/125 s, process CN-16, Status M. x axis is log H in lux-seconds as printed; b (top), g, r (lowest) are the three solid curves, drawn from log H about -3.6 to 0.4 only. The Blue/Green/Red labels are rotated text between the curves and were not masked.", "Spectral sensitivity: the y axis (titled 'Relative Sensitivity* (log)') has no numbers, only a scale arrow marking 1.0 (log unit) between two horizontal rules; y is therefore relative log sensitivity with 0 set at the lower rule and 1.0 at the upper rule (absolute offset not printed). Sensitivity is the reciprocal of the exposure (J/cm2) required to produce a specified density, which the sheet gives as 1.0 above D-min. Three layers only. b runs about 400-507 nm, g about 461-598 nm, r about 565-674 nm; each curve ends in near-vertical drops, so the last few nm are approximated on the 5 nm grid.", "Spectral dye density plot has only 'mid' (Mid-scale Density: typical densities for a mid-scale neutral subject) and 'min' (Minimum Density), no separate c/m/y dyes; the curves run about 400-720 nm.", "Process: the plots say CN-16 (Fuji's name); page 3 says Vista films are process-compatible and are developed in AP 70 / CN-16 / C-41. Film speed ISO 200/24 deg daylight (tungsten 3200 K: ISO 50 with Wratten 80A or LBB-12). The sheet says 'when printed on AgfaPhoto papers' without naming a paper."]}}